<h3>四、Remediation Timeline</h3>
<table cellpadding=5 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #ccc;width:100%">
<tr><td><b>November 15</b></td><td>Fund payment received + remediation plan submitted</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>December 1</b></td><td>Re-audit (safety findings)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>January 15</b></td><td>Re-audit (wage & overtime compliance)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>February 1</b></td><td>Environmental compliance verification</td></tr>
<tr style="background:#fff3cd"><td><b>March 1</b></td><td style="color:#e00"><b>If not remediated: Production suspension + public disclosure</b></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>五、Consequences of Non-Compliance</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Production suspension:</b> All Adidas orders halted</li>
<li><b>Public disclosure:</b> Listed on Adidas "Non-Compliant Suppliers" page (public)</li>
<li><b>Contract termination:</b> Your supply agreement (valued at $4.2M/year) will be terminated</li>
<li><b>Industry blacklist:</b> Shared with Fair Labor Association (FLA) members</li>
</ul>

<h3>六、About This Audit</h3>
<p>We understand that an unexpected "compliance fund" payment may raise questions — this is a healthy skepticism. However:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Compliance Improvement Fund is mandated by the Adidas Supplier Code (Section 7.4, effective 2024)</li>
<li>Funds are used to compensate affected workers and improve factory conditions</li>
<li>Similar funds are operated by Nike (Fair Labor Fund), H&M (Wage Management Fund), and Patagonia</li>
<li>Payment to the Asia-Pacific entity (rather than the German HQ) is required under Singapore's regulatory framework for cross-border compliance payments</li>
</ul>

